CAT = "ea1Prelim"; SEG = "PRE"
# Preliminary work and taxpayer data (SEE Part 1 domain 1). Tax year 2025 unless stated.
Q = [
("Before preparing a returning client's 2025 return, why should a preparer review the client's 2024 return?",
 "To pick up carryovers", ["To copy the filing status", "To reuse last year's W-2s", "To skip the intake interview"],
 "The prior return shows items that carry forward, such as a capital loss, charitable contribution or passive loss carryover, and gives a baseline to spot missing income. Filing status and documents must be rechecked each year."),
("A client's 2024 Schedule D shows a $5,200 capital loss carryover to 2025. On the 2025 return the carryover keeps:",
 "Its short- or long-term character", ["No character at all", "Ordinary loss treatment", "Only half of its amount"],
 "A capital loss carryover retains its character as short-term or long-term and is netted with the next year's gains and losses."),
("A client received an Identity Protection PIN from the IRS for 2025. When is it needed?",
 "On any 2025 return filed", ["Only when filing on paper", "Only for an amended return", "Only when calling the IRS"],
 "An IP PIN is a six-digit number valid for one calendar year. It must be entered on any return filed for that year, or the e-filed return rejects and a paper return is delayed."),
("Who may be issued an Individual Taxpayer Identification Number (ITIN)?",
 "Someone not eligible for an SSN", ["Any U.S. citizen without a job", "An employer that has no EIN yet", "A child who already has an SSN"],
 "An ITIN, applied for on Form W-7, is for a person who needs a U.S. taxpayer number but cannot get a Social Security number, such as a nonresident alien with a filing requirement or a dependent who is not SSN-eligible."),
("Paula, a foreign national, was in the U.S. 120 days in 2025, 180 days in 2024 and 180 days in 2023. Under the substantial presence test her weighted day count is:",
 "210 days", ["120 days", "180 days", "150 days"],
 "Count all 2025 days, one third of 2024 days and one sixth of 2023 days: 120 + 60 + 30 = 210. With at least 31 days in 2025 and 183 or more weighted days, she meets the test."),
("Under the substantial presence test, an individual must be present in the U.S. for at least how many days in the current year?",
 "31", ["183", "60", "122"],
 "The test needs at least 31 days in the current year and 183 weighted days over the three-year period. The 183-day figure is the three-year total, not the current-year minimum."),
("A foreign national obtained a green card on March 1, 2025. For 2025 she is:",
 "A dual-status alien", ["A nonresident for the year", "A resident for all of 2025", "Exempt from U.S. tax"],
 "Under the green card test, residency begins on the first day she is present as a lawful permanent resident. Before that she is a nonresident unless she met the substantial presence test, so the year is dual status unless an election applies."),
("Dev, a U.S. citizen, is married to Lena, a nonresident alien with no U.S. income. How can they file a joint 2025 return?",
 "Elect resident status for Lena", ["They may not file jointly", "File Form 1040-NR together", "Attach Form 8379 to the return"],
 "A U.S. citizen or resident married to a nonresident alien can make the section 6013(g) election to treat the spouse as a resident for the whole year. Lena's worldwide income then becomes taxable."),
("A calendar-year individual living in the U.S. files Form 4868 for 2025. The extension gives:",
 "More time to file, not to pay", ["More time to file and to pay", "Six more months to pay only", "Relief from all late penalties"],
 "Form 4868 extends the filing date to October 15, 2026. Tax not paid by April 15, 2026 still accrues interest and the late payment penalty."),
("A U.S. citizen whose tax home and abode are outside the U.S. and Puerto Rico on the regular due date gets an automatic extension to file to:",
 "June 15", ["October 15", "May 15", "April 30"],
 "Citizens and residents living and working abroad get an automatic two-month extension to June 15, though interest runs from April 15 on unpaid tax. Form 4868 can extend further to October 15."),
("A taxpayer lives in a county covered by an IRS disaster postponement. For a filing deadline that falls inside the postponement period, the taxpayer:",
 "May file by the new IRS date", ["Must still file on April 15", "Must request it on Form 4868", "Loses the extension if late"],
 "When the IRS announces disaster relief, deadlines falling in the period are postponed automatically for taxpayers in the covered area. No form is needed to claim it."),
("Nora's husband's share of a 2025 joint refund is expected to be taken for his past-due student loan. Nora had her own wages withheld. She may protect her share by filing:",
 "Form 8379, injured spouse", ["Form 8857, innocent spouse", "Form 4868 for an extension", "Form 8332 with the return"],
 "Form 8379 lets the injured spouse claim the part of a joint refund that belongs to her when it would otherwise be offset for the other spouse's past-due debts. Form 8857 is for relief from joint tax liability."),
("Marital status for the 2025 tax year is determined as of:",
 "December 31, 2025", ["January 1, 2025", "The day the return is filed", "April 15, 2026"],
 "A taxpayer married on the last day of the year is treated as married for the whole year. A divorce final by December 31 makes the person unmarried for the year."),
("Ivan's wife died in March 2025. He has not remarried. For 2025 he may file:",
 "A joint return with his wife", ["Only as single", "As qualifying surviving spouse", "Only married filing separately"],
 "A surviving spouse may file a joint return for the year of death if not remarried by year end. Qualifying surviving spouse status covers the two years after the year of death, with a dependent child."),
("Rosa is married but has lived apart from her husband since May 2025. She paid over half the cost of the home where her son lived all year. She may file as:",
 "Head of household", ["Single", "Married filing jointly only", "Qualifying surviving spouse"],
 "A married person is considered unmarried for head of household if the spouse did not live in the home during the last six months of the year and the taxpayer kept up a home for a qualifying child for over half the year."),
("A single taxpayer pays more than half the cost of keeping up a separate home for his mother, his dependent. For head of household he must show:",
 "He paid over half its upkeep", ["She lived with him all year", "She is under age 65", "She has no income at all"],
 "A dependent parent does not need to live with the taxpayer. Head of household is allowed if the taxpayer pays more than half the cost of the parent's main home, such as a home or care facility."),
("Ana and her mother both could claim Ana's 7-year-old son, who lived with both of them all year in the same home. If Ana's mother wants to claim him, she may do so only if:",
 "Her AGI is higher than Ana's", ["She paid more of his support", "She is the older taxpayer", "Ana agrees in writing"],
 "When a parent can claim the child but does not, a non-parent may claim the child only if the non-parent's AGI is higher than the highest AGI of any parent who could claim the child."),
("Divorced parents share custody. The child spent 200 nights with the mother and 165 with the father. Under the tie-breaker rules, the custodial parent is:",
 "The mother", ["The father", "The parent with the higher AGI", "Whichever parent pays more"],
 "The custodial parent is the one the child lived with for the greater number of nights. AGI decides only when the nights are equal."),
("A noncustodial parent may claim the child tax credit for a child of divorced parents when the custodial parent signs:",
 "Form 8332", ["Form 2120", "Form 8379", "Form 8814"],
 "Form 8332, or a similar statement, releases the custodial parent's claim to the exemption and the child tax credit. It does not transfer head of household status, the dependent care credit or the earned income credit."),
("Four siblings support their father, who lives alone. No one sibling provides over half. A multiple support agreement lets one of them claim him if that sibling provided over:",
 "10% of his support", ["25% of his support", "50% of his support", "5% of his support"],
 "Under a multiple support agreement, the group must provide over half the support, and the person claiming must have provided over 10%. The others who gave over 10% sign Form 2120 statements."),
("Which person can never be a taxpayer's qualifying relative, even with full support?",
 "Anyone's qualifying child", ["An unrelated member of household", "A father-in-law", "A nephew living elsewhere"],
 "A person who is the qualifying child of any taxpayer cannot be someone else's qualifying relative. In-laws and nephews qualify by relationship, and an unrelated person can qualify by living with the taxpayer all year."),
("A child who otherwise qualifies must have a valid Social Security number issued by the due date of the return for the taxpayer to claim:",
 "The child tax credit", ["The credit for other dependents", "Head of household status", "The dependent care credit"],
 "The child tax credit requires the child's SSN valid for employment, issued before the return's due date. A child with an ITIN may still qualify the taxpayer for the credit for other dependents."),
("A dependent must generally be a U.S. citizen, U.S. national or resident of the U.S. or which other country?",
 "Canada or Mexico", ["Any NATO member", "Any treaty country", "The UK or Ireland"],
 "The citizen or resident test is met by a U.S. citizen, U.S. national, or a resident of the U.S., Canada or Mexico. An adopted child living with a U.S. citizen parent also qualifies."),
("Self-employment income alone creates a 2025 filing requirement when net earnings from self-employment are at least:",
 "$400", ["$600", "$1,000", "$10,000"],
 "A taxpayer with $400 or more of net earnings from self-employment must file to report self-employment tax, even when income is below the standard deduction. The $600 figure is the Form 1099-NEC reporting point."),
("Jo, 15, has $4,800 of interest and dividends and no earned income. Her parents are alive and do not make the Form 8814 election. Her unearned income above the threshold is taxed:",
 "On Form 8615 at parent rates", ["At her own rates only", "On Schedule B of her parents", "Only once she turns 18"],
 "The kiddie tax taxes a child's net unearned income above the threshold at the parent's rate, using Form 8615. Parents may instead elect Form 8814 to report it on their own return only when the child's income is solely interest and dividends under a limit."),
("A parent elects on Form 8814 to report a child's interest and dividends. A result of the election is that:",
 "The child need not file", ["The child gets a refund", "The kiddie tax no longer applies", "The parent's AGI goes down"],
 "With the Form 8814 election, the child's income is reported on the parent's return and the child does not file. The parent's AGI rises, which can reduce credits and deductions based on AGI."),
("Leah received advance premium tax credit for Marketplace coverage in 2025. To reconcile it she must:",
 "File a return with Form 8962", ["Do nothing if under 65", "File only if she owes tax", "Attach Form 1095-C only"],
 "Anyone who received advance payments of the premium tax credit must file a return and reconcile on Form 8962 using Form 1095-A, even if not otherwise required to file."),
("For the premium tax credit, household income is the modified AGI of:",
 "Tax family members required to file", ["Only the taxpayer", "Every person living in the home", "Only those enrolled in the policy"],
 "Household income adds the modified AGI of the taxpayer, spouse and dependents who are required to file a return. Other people living in the home do not count."),
("A client paid a nanny wages above the 2025 household employee threshold. Which additional item must be filed with her Form 1040?",
 "Schedule H", ["Schedule SE", "Form 941", "Form 2441 only"],
 "Household employment taxes for a nanny are reported on Schedule H with Form 1040. The employer also gives the nanny a Form W-2."),
("A client received a CP2000 notice proposing tax on a Form 1099-B sale left off the return. The notice is:",
 "A proposed change, not a bill", ["A final assessment of tax", "An audit at an IRS office", "A notice of federal tax lien"],
 "A CP2000 proposes changes from information return matching. The taxpayer can agree or respond with documents, such as the stock's basis, before any tax is assessed."),
("A client says a U.S. bank account and a foreign brokerage account both exist. Which accounts count toward the FBAR $10,000 aggregate?",
 "Only the foreign account", ["Both of the accounts", "Only the U.S. bank account", "Neither account"],
 "The FBAR covers financial accounts located outside the U.S. Domestic accounts are never included, even at a foreign-owned bank's U.S. branch."),
("A client sold stock and got a Form 1099-B showing proceeds but no basis. The preparer should:",
 "Get the basis from her records", ["Report a basis of zero", "Leave the sale off the return", "Use the proceeds as basis"],
 "Gain is proceeds minus basis. When the broker does not report basis, the preparer obtains it from purchase records or statements and reports it on Form 8949, keeping support in the file."),
]
